
  Notifications are the last layer of that stack, not the first. I'd rather not design a delivery system on top of a
  model I've invented on your behalf.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether this is a single-user page in a browser or a multi-user app with a server. That decision is not
  cheap to reverse — it determines whether "notify" means re-rendering a badge in the DOM, or persisted per-user
  subscriptions, a change feed, and delivery channels (in-app, email, push). Almost every later choice inherits from it.

  - [1mSingle-user, client-side[0m — tasks live in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, notifications are in-page toasts or a bell menu. Cheap, no
    backend, works offline. Rules out notifying anyone but the person at the keyboard, and rules out delivery when the
    tab is closed.
  - [1mMulti-user with a backend[0m — real accounts, shared tasks, server-owned event log. Makes "users" plural and
    meaningful, opens email/push later. Costs a server, a database, auth, and deployment before a single notification
    fires.
  - [1mDecompose first[0m — spec tasks + users now, notifications as the next cycle. Slowest to a visible notification, but
    nothing gets designed against a guessed model.

  My recommendation is [1mdecompose first[0m, then build toward multi-user. The notifications design will be mostly guesswork
  until a task and a user are concrete things, and guessed foundations are the expensive kind to redo.
